 so once  sequencing became cheap and easy and  ways to computationally align viruses  much much data has come out of  associating viruses or changes in the  virum with disease but we were still  really left with correlative data the  question we talked or asked was what  does the virum do to the innate immune  system in health and what does it do in  the context of inflammatory bowel  disease  so I think our data has really clearly  shown in both the reductionist system  where we just put the viruses we  isolated from the intestinal colon on  human macrophages in a dish the healthy  viruses very much provoked an  anti-inflammatory pathway which was an  atypical viral response so it wasn't a  type 1 interferon-induced response  actually that if anything that was down  regulated and instead what was up  regulated was this pro-survival  um  anti-inflammatory pathway and in stark  contrast the IBD viruses are very much  provoked inflammation so I think we can  confidently say  um from the human macrophage experiments  that a reductionist system viruses on  their own can actually  trigger inflammation so the IBD virus  trigger inflammation and then even in  Vivo when we humanize those mice very  much the healthy virome was protective  whereas the IBD virum provoked  inflammation and actually even  spontaneously provoked that expansion of  those cx3 cr1 positive mononuclear  phagocytes  so I think uh we're at a juncture here  in Byron research I think we've now  shown that the immune system certainly  doesn't ignore these viruses in health  and these viruses that are altered can  contribute to disease phenotype  um the questions you know we have moving  forward is really  um is it that it's the change in the  virum specifically is its certain  appearance of viruses such as the  introvirus B family that's really  triggering this inflammation that's a  very very likely hypothesis or outcome  but the same  um or sort of a separate hypothesis is  really that whether the same viruses  could be doing different things  depending on the immune State and we've  seen this for certain bacteria where a  so-called symbiont can become a  pathobion in the context of inflammation  and so we're very interested in this Con  in this concept of whether the IBD virum  triggers inflammation because of the  appearance of enteroviruses and other  phage that aren't normally seen in  healthy state but at the same breath do  some normally healthy viruses such as  Anella viruses say actually start being  pro-inflammatory in a prime inflammatory  environment and these are the types of  questions that we'll be asking moving  forward  I think what was really convincing for  me was this a genetic Association of the  virus receptor mda5 with IBD so we  showed quite clearly that that is a loss  of function mutation in mda5 that  Associates with IBD if we took um  intestinal epithelial cells from a  patient that it was a compound  heterozygote for that loss of function  clearly there was detrimental  consequences for that so you really do  need a functional mda5 receptor  sensing the healthy virum and really  when you lose that you can actually  there's detrimental consequences for the  intestinal epithelial cells  so I think collectively our work really  does show that the virum is not ignored  the healthy virus certainly is not  ignored and in a similar way to the  microbiome actually works to educate our  immune system and and keep sort of our  intestine  um in a healthy state by provoking sort  of these anti-inflammatory pro-survival  Pathways and it can do that autonomous  autonomously to the microbiome  um and in contrast the IBD virum very  much provokes inflammation  and so the idea here is you know well  can we take this a step forward on the  one hand can we start to harness the  healthy virum in a similar way to the  microbiome to sort of suppress  inflammation in IBD  but then on the flip side what can we do  about the IBD viruses and so can we  start to try and eliminate some of these  viruses in IBD that we think are  contributing to this inflammatory uh  phenotype and I think this will be the  really the the next Frontier in  environment research  um and so with that obviously I get to  tell all of you about the work we've  done  um this is the been the hard work of of  many people in my lab but particularly  uh Fatima adiliagdam who was just a  stellar postdoc who really LED this  project Fatima is now training to be a  surgeon herself so after all this work  looking at colon resection she's now  actually getting to do some surgery  herself so I look forward to having her  as a physician  um surgery scientist colleague in the  future I want to point out hajira  amatula another postdoc in the lab as  well as Isabella and really everyone in  the Jeffrey lab that helped us with this  project this was a new frontier for my  lab so really collaborations were key I  want to point out particularly Scott  Hanley who really again I just want to  emphasize is really leading the field in  firearm computational analysis and we  couldn't have identified all those  viruses in those surgery resections  without him  um I also want to give a shout out to  Romney Xavier and Richard Xavier at MGH  has set up an enormous  um uh registry of IBD patients at Mass  General Hospital so it allows us  researchers access to tissues blood  um whatever we like biopsies uh from  these um uh genotyped individuals which  has been just such a fantastic resource  to try and understand inflammatory bowel  disease and of course computational help  and as well as John ryu's team in  Montreal who made the intestinal  epithelial organoids from us from these  mda5 loss of function patients  um so I'm obviously not there live thank  you everyone for your attention what I  will say is that I'm very happy to be  answering questions via email so if you  have questions about this work and the  field more generally please reach out  and I'll respond as soon as I can and  thank you uh for your attention  I want to thank you Dr Jeffrey for your  time today and for your important  research and before we go I want to  thank our audience for joining us today  as well questions submitted today and  during the on-demand period will be  addressed by our speaker via the contact  information you provided at the time of  registration and this webcast can be  viewed on demand for two years until  September 7th  2024. lab rates will alert you via email  when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time take  care everyone bye-bye

